! ============================================================
! 02c  Two independent routes to the same Hessian  (run both)
! ============================================================
! Second derivatives can be obtained two ways:
!   analytical -- solve the coupled-perturbed SCF equations
!   numerical  -- finite differences of the analytical gradient
! They are different code paths through different mathematics
! and they should agree.  Running both is the cheapest
! independent check available on a frequency calculation, and
! it costs you nothing but a second run.
!
! EXPECTED: the two agree to a few tenths of a cm^-1 on the
! stiff modes.  Soft modes -- anything below ~100 cm^-1 -- are
! the ones to watch: their force constants are small, so a
! fixed absolute error in the Hessian is a large RELATIVE
! error there.  For weakly bound complexes the two routes can
! differ by several cm^-1 on the softest modes.
!
! The numerical route costs 6N SCF+gradient evaluations, so it
! is the slower of the two, but it needs no CPSCF and is
! therefore the fallback whenever the analytical route is
! unavailable or suspect.
%method  BP86
%basis   6-31G(d)
%job     opt+freq
%hess_method analytical
%grid    fine
%charge  0
%mult    1

%geo
 O   0.0000000   0.0000000   0.1173000
 H   0.0000000   0.7572000  -0.4692000
 H   0.0000000  -0.7572000  -0.4692000
